---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/content/Placeholder.astro';
import PipelineExplorer from '../components/science/PipelineExplorer.astro';
import { pipeline, statusLabel } from '../data/pipeline';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'six-steps', title: 'Six steps, one breath' },
  { id: 'controls', title: 'Controls travel with every sample' },
  { id: 'where-it-can-fail', title: 'Where the chain can break' },
];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="how-it-works"
  toc={toc}
  brief={[
    'Six steps take a breath sample to a pattern, and a pattern to a referral for standard clinical assessment.',
    'Every step carries its own uncertainty, and each one is marked with where the project actually stands.',
    'Controls matter as much as samples: room air, blanks and reference standards are part of every measurement.',
  ]}
>
  <Section
    id="six-steps"
    title="Six steps, one breath"
    lead="Open a step to see what happens, what we would have to demonstrate, and what is still an open question."
  >
    <PipelineExplorer />
  </Section>

  <Section id="controls" title="Controls travel with every sample">
    <p>
      A breath measurement is only meaningful next to the things it is compared with. Three controls are part of the
      design rather than an afterthought, because without them a signal cannot be separated from the room, the device or
      the cells themselves.
    </p>

    <Table label="Controls planned for every measurement">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Control</th>
            <th scope="col">What it rules out</th>
            <th scope="col">When it is measured</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Room air blank</th>
            <td>Compounds that come from the environment rather than the person</td>
            <td>Alongside each breath sample</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Unexposed modules</th>
            <td>Background reporter signal and drift in the cells themselves</td>
            <td>Every run, same batch of cells</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Known compound standard</th>
            <td>Loss of module sensitivity between batches or over time</td>
            <td>Every run, at a fixed concentration</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>

    <Note kind="context">
      <p>
        The experimental controls used in the laboratory work are described in{' '}
        <a href={href('experiments')}>Experiments</a>, where they are tied to specific hypotheses.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="where-it-can-fail" title="Where the chain can break">
    <p>
      Each step depends on the one before it. If sampling is inconsistent, nothing downstream can recover; if the
      modules respond to everything equally, the pattern carries no information. This is the honest status of each link.
    </p>

    <Table label="Status of each step in the chain">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Step</th>
            <th scope="col">Where the project stands</th>
            <th scope="col">Biggest open question</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            pipeline.map((step, i) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {i + 1}. {step.short}
                </th>
                <td>{statusLabel[step.status]}</td>
                <td>{step.openQuestions[0]}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>

    <Placeholder title="Device and cartridge design, if the team builds hardware" where="src/pages/how-it-works.astro">
      <ul>
        <li>How the sample reaches the modules in a physical prototype.</li>
        <li>How the reporter signal is read, and with what optics.</li>
        <li>Photographs or CAD renderings of the prototype.</li>
      </ul>
    </Placeholder>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>
